Once again, a PC-related semi-technical article. Please bear with us;
as you know, one of NetHack's parents was a PC-based game and there
are many PC-based players. Keeping a programme of this size running on
a machine that small is proving to be of comparable difficulty to
winning pl 5 ('They say it can be done....'), and only by pooling our
knowledge can we guarantee success.... Think of it as tech.spoilers!

In article <1989Dec8.173104.756@bdmrrr.bdm.com> berry@bdmrrr.bdm.com (Brian M. Berry) writes:
>	I just got a hold of Nethack 3.0 (pl 6) sources just a bit ago (thanks
>to some helpful people participating in the newsgroup) and have ported them
>to my 286 PC at home.  Reading through the material on porting, I've gotten
>the impression that perhaps the development team, and everyone else for that
>matter, have essentially "given up" on clean Turbo C ports.

Basically what happened is this: since the executable is in the 800K
range we KNEW we needed overlays. Microsoft has an overlay linker;
Borland has not. So we worked with the Microsoft tools.

When we finished we tested with the Microsoft linker and Turbo C (an
unusual configuration, but there are a few people who MIGHT have it).
It didn't work and the diagnostics were Strange and Weird. So,
figuring it wasn't very important anyway (most people with Turbo
presumably use tlink, which doesn't support overlays), we turned out
attention to performance, which is now our main concern.

BUT if anyone HAS made headway with Turbo C and ovlmgr.asm, please let
me know.

>Full option
>compiles are not practical because the overlay manager included only works
>with Microsoft C.

Please note that the overlay manager included does NOT interact with C
in any way (well, ok, it assumes that the programme uses the
terminate-with-return-code exit method and doesn't longjmp between
overlays, but that should be about the limit of it), it ONLY has hooks
into the linker; and it really is a very well-behaved (if ugly) bit of
code, all things considered. Any Strange Interactions going on are
between C and the linker, not between C and us, as far as we can see.

So, no, it is NOT possible for tlink to support overlays. It is
THEORETICALLY possible to support Turbo C .OBJ modules with the MS
Overlay Linker, but... we get Mystic Errors.

Either we need to figure out what's going on in there (which doesn't
seem either easy or likely), or.... Nah, I don't think I'm up for
writing a new linker tonight....
